# Map Card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The map card shows where people, devices and zones are on a map. In YAML it is `type: map`. Use it to give a rough sense of where things are, never as the only record. Every location also needs a line of text.

This page owns the card and its limits. The pattern for location records is in [Maps, zones and location records](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html), which also has a working OpenStreetMap example. The Civica map colours are in [Map colours](../styles/colour-maps.html).

This page covers:

- Map Card
  - `hui-map-card`
  - `ha-map`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

From top to bottom:

1. An optional title.
2. The map, with a zoom control, markers for entities and circles for zones.
3. A “Reset focus” button, and a “Toggle grouping” button when there is more than one marker.
4. In a panel view, an overview with tabs for People, Devices and Zones, and a “No recent activity” message when there is nothing to list.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-map-card"><code>hui-map-card</code></h3>

```yaml
type: map
title: Household zones
entities:
  - person.sam
  - zone.home
  - zone.school
default_zoom: 14
theme_mode: auto
```

| Key | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | Text above the map |
| `entities` | Entities to plot; each can set `label_mode` (`name`, `state`, `attribute` or `icon`), `name`, `color` and `focus` |
| `geo_location_sources` | Sources of geolocated events to plot |
| `aspect_ratio` | The shape of the map, for example `16:9` |
| `default_zoom` | Starting zoom; default 14 |
| `auto_fit` and `fit_zones` | Fit the view to the markers or the zones |
| `show_all` and `show_zone_radius` | Show every entity and zone circles |
| `hours_to_show` | Draws paths for the period; default 0, so no paths |
| `theme_mode` | `auto`, `light` or `dark` |
| `map_style` | A preset or a custom style (see below) |
| `cluster` | Group markers that are close together |
| `scale_ruler` | Show a scale ruler |
| `conditions` | Show the card only when conditions are met |

`dark_mode` still works but is deprecated; use `theme_mode`.

**Map styles.** `map_style` offers six presets: `default`, `colorful`, `natural`, `muted`, `gray` and `toner`. A custom style can set `base`, `colors` and `colors_dark`, among other options. The dark colours replace the light colours; they are not merged with them. The card’s own colours win over any theme.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The card surface, the title, and the text and divider colours. The theme does not recolour the map: land, water and roads keep Home Assistant’s colours, because the Civica theme sets none of the `--ha-color-map-*` variables. The [Map colours](../styles/colour-maps.html) page describes the palette used in documentation examples, not a theme setting.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** The marker shapes, the zone circles, the controls and the tiles. Markers use black text on a light map and white on a dark one.

**What needs the card-mod integration.** Test any card-mod selector in your own dashboard.

<h3 id="child-ha-map"><code>ha-map</code></h3>

The map element inside the card. A dashboard author cannot place it alone.

What it does:

- It draws a vector map with MapLibre. On a device that cannot show a vector map it falls back to a simpler Leaflet map, which cannot be edited.
- Tiles come from the Map tiles integration. Without a connection the map sets up without tiles.
- It is 300 pixels tall by default.
- It names markers from their titles. A marker that can be focused is a button. A titled marker that cannot be focused is exposed as an image, and a marker with no title is hidden from assistive technology.
- A zone circle on an interactive map opens its more info when you click it, or press Enter or Space on it.
- In dark mode, Home Assistant applies a colour filter to the map. In light mode there is none.
- The zoom control sits top left, or top right in a panel view.

Some people may not be able to use the map at all, so give every location in text.

A theme author can recolour the vector map with the `--ha-color-map-land`, `-water`, `-green`, `-area`, `-building`, `-road`, `-transit`, `-boundary` and `-label` variables, among others.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One native card shows people, devices and zones together, with the map style you choose.
- The map has no text description. Without a list beside it, people who cannot use a map get nothing.
- Pinch and drag are the main ways to move the map, and the zoom buttons are the single-pointer alternative.
- The map needs a connection to the Map tiles integration to draw tiles.
- Markers can reveal where people are. Choose who and what to plot with care.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Say what the map shows, when it was last updated, and how many items are plotted compared with how many are listed. Put a table or list beside the map with the name, state and time of every location, including ones with no coordinates. Keep the number of markers small. Check privacy first: see [Presence, people and shared-screen privacy](../home-assistant/presence-and-privacy.html). Do not use colour as the only way to tell zones or people apart.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: the map needs a text alternative, which is the list beside it.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the list carries the same facts as the map.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: label each marker or zone in words.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: check the map and the panel overview at narrow widths.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap: test panning, zooming and focus in your own dashboard.
- 2.5.1 Pointer Gestures and 2.5.7 Dragging Movements: use the zoom buttons as the single-pointer way.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: marker names come from entity titles, so give them clear names.

